@id("close-workorder-main-after-human-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"MaintData___close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Vantry___get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    eventPrincipal: principal,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    output.decision: Drupe::Vantry___get_approval_Output_decision::"ACCEPTED",
    output.approver_kind: Drupe::Vantry___get_approval_Output_approver_kind::"PERSON"
  }
};

// Read-only lookups. Vantry___get_approval must itself be permitted, or its
// responses are never recorded and the close policy can never be satisfied.
@id("allow-read-only-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"Vantry___get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"Yarbeck___get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_failure_codes"
  ],
  resource
);
```

The agent can close a work order only at MAIN, and only after a completed `Vantry___get_approval` call in this session, within the last hour. That call must have returned `ACCEPTED` with approver kind `PERSON`, for the same `site_id` and `wonum` as the close request.

I did not use `Yarbeck___get_approval` as proof of approval. Its outputs are free-form strings with no declared vocabulary, so I can't tell what a person's approval looks like there. The agent may still call it as a read-only lookup. If Yarbeck uses the same `ACCEPTED` / `PERSON` values, it could be added as a second approval source, but that would need a separate policy because temporal blocks have no `||`.

Approving, assigning, cancelling, generating and updating work orders have no permit, so they are denied by default. The agent therefore cannot approve its own work.

The match on the `decision` and `approver_kind` enum values uses entity literals (for example `Drupe::Vantry___get_approval_Output_decision::"ACCEPTED"`). The reference shows plain string literals for output fields, so if the validator rejects the entity form, switch those two patterns to `"ACCEPTED"` and `"PERSON"`.

